[[:db/retract 17592186152764 :question/answer-fields 17592186151091] [:db/retract 17592186152805 :question/answer-fields 17592186151337] {:db/id 17592186152191 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-2c6a7c2f-ebd8-5018-a687-121a9ed1a14a"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id 17592186151993 :question/problem "Find the greatest common divisor of $713$ and $322.$" :question/worked-solution "We use the Euclidean algorithm, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{aligned}713 & = & 322(2) & + & 69 \\\\  & ↙ &  & ↙ &  \\\\ 322 & = & 69(4) & + & 46 \\\\  & ↙ &  & ↙ &  \\\\ 69 & = & 46(1) & + & 23 \\\\  & ↙ &  & ↙ &  \\\\ 46 & = & 23(2) & + & 0\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe last positive remainder is ${\\color{red}23}.$ Therefore, we conclude that the greatest common divisor of $713$ and $322$ is $23.$ Symbolically, we write\n\n$$\n\\textrm{gcd}(713, 322) = 23.\n$$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186152166 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-ee6022bc-35f0-5e0b-b95e-866627e7670f"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id 17592186151957 :question/problem "Find the greatest common divisor of $1\\,036$ and $555.$" :question/worked-solution "We use the Euclidean algorithm, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{aligned}1036 & = & 555(1) & + & 481 \\\\  & ↙ &  & ↙ &  \\\\ 555 & = & 481(1) & + & 74 \\\\  & ↙ &  & ↙ &  \\\\ 481 & = & 74(6) & + & 37 \\\\  & ↙ &  & ↙ &  \\\\ 74 & = & 37(2) & + & 0\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe last positive remainder is ${\\color{red}37}.$ Therefore, we conclude that the greatest common divisor of $1\\,036$ and $555$ is $37.$ Symbolically, we write\n\n$$\n\\textrm{gcd}(1\\,036, 555) = 37.\n$$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186152223 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-3936dcf8-3c18-5d25-b1ef-fee251049b9e" "question-9e2b9768-6303-5aa7-8a02-db48065ae94a"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id 17592186152033 :question/problem "Which of the following statements are true?\n\n- $\\textrm{gcd}(651, 574) = 7$\n\n- $\\textrm{gcd}(651, 574) = 1$\n\n- $651$ and $574$ are coprime" :question/worked-solution "Recall that two integers are coprime if their greatest common divisor is $1.$ \n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true, while statement II is false. Indeed, let's use the Euclidean algorithm, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{aligned}651 & = & 574\\cdot 1 & + & 77 \\\\  & ↙ &  & ↙ &  \\\\ 574 & = & 77\\cdot 7 & + & 35 \\\\  & ↙ &  & ↙ &  \\\\ 77 & = & 35\\cdot 2 & + & 7 \\\\  & ↙ &  & ↙ &  \\\\ 35 & = & 7\\cdot 5 & + & 0\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe last positive remainder is ${\\color{blue}7}.$ So, the greatest common divisor of $651$ and $574$ is $7\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\textrm{gcd}(651, 574) = 7.\n$$\n\n- Statement III is false. Since $\\textrm{gcd}(651, 574) > 1,$ the numbers are not coprime.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\"" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186152764 :question/problem "Find the greatest common divisor of $296$ and $185.$" :question/worked-solution "We use the Euclidean algorithm, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{aligned}296 & = & 185(1) & + & 111 \\\\  & ↙ &  & ↙ &  \\\\ 185 & = & 111(1) & + & 74 \\\\  & ↙ &  & ↙ &  \\\\ 111 & = & 74(1) & + & 37 \\\\  & ↙ &  & ↙ &  \\\\ 74 & = & 37(2) & + & 0\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe last positive remainder is $37.$ Therefore, we conclude that the greatest common divisor of $296$ and $185$ is $37.$ Symbolically, we write\n\n$$\n\\text{gcd}(296,185)=37.\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-2f564e11-e3e9-51f3-8747-8c068bd5977d"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-2f564e11-e3e9-51f3-8747-8c068bd5977d" :answer-field/id #uuid "2f564e11-e3e9-51f3-8747-8c068bd5977d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-e24dd563-bb32-5701-9d59-c54cef